# Span&lt;T&gt;, Memory&lt;T&gt;, and Zero-Allocation Code

`Span<T>` is a view over contiguous memory: a pointer and a length. Slicing one allocates nothing, because there is nothing new to allocate.

## Slicing without copying

```csharp
string line = "2026-09-03,checkout,1299.00";

// Allocating version: Split creates an array plus three strings
var parts = line.Split(',');
var amount = decimal.Parse(parts[2]);

// Zero-allocation version: spans into the original string
ReadOnlySpan<char> span = line;
int first = span.IndexOf(',');
int second = span[(first + 1)..].IndexOf(',') + first + 1;

ReadOnlySpan<char> date = span[..first];
ReadOnlySpan<char> kind = span[(first + 1)..second];
decimal total = decimal.Parse(span[(second + 1)..]);
```

Every modern parse API in .NET has a span overload: `int.Parse`, `decimal.TryParse`, `DateTime.TryParseExact`, `Utf8Parser`. Parsing a million-line CSV this way removes millions of intermediate strings.

## Stack allocation for small buffers

```csharp
Span<char> buffer = stackalloc char[64];       // no heap, freed with the stack frame
if (value.TryFormat(buffer, out int written))
    Console.WriteLine(buffer[..written].ToString());

Span<byte> key = stackalloc byte[32];
System.Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator.Fill(key);
```

Keep `stackalloc` small and constant, a few hundred bytes at most, and never inside a loop. The stack is roughly 1 MB per thread and overflowing it kills the process outright, with no catchable exception.

## Span rules, and what Memory<T> is for

`Span<T>` is a `ref struct`. The compiler guarantees it never escapes to the heap, which means it **cannot**:

- be a field of a class,
- be captured by a lambda or an iterator,
- cross an `await`.

That last one matters constantly, so async code uses `Memory<T>` instead:

```csharp
static async Task<int> ReadChunkAsync(Stream stream, Memory<byte> buffer, CancellationToken ct)
{
    int read = await stream.ReadAsync(buffer, ct);   // Memory survives the await
    Span<byte> data = buffer.Span[..read];           // convert to Span for the sync work
    return data.IndexOf((byte)'\n');
}
```

`Memory<T>` is the heap-safe counterpart; call `.Span` to get a `Span<T>` for the synchronous stretch between awaits.

## Pooling large buffers

```csharp
var pool = System.Buffers.ArrayPool<byte>.Shared;
byte[] buffer = pool.Rent(64 * 1024);
try
{
    int read = stream.Read(buffer, 0, buffer.Length);
    Process(buffer.AsSpan(0, read));
}
finally
{
    pool.Return(buffer);      // always return, ideally clearArray: true for sensitive data
}
```

`ArrayPool<T>` recycles large arrays instead of pushing them through the LOH on every request. The `finally` is mandatory: a rented array that is never returned is a leak that the GC cannot help you with.

> [!NOTE]
> `string.AsSpan()` is free; `span.ToString()` allocates. The win comes from staying in span-land for the whole pipeline. A "zero-allocation" parser that calls `ToString()` at the end of every row has simply moved the allocation, not removed it.

# Architecture: Layering, CQRS, and Domain Boundaries

## The dependency rule

Layered ("clean", "onion", "hexagonal") architectures differ in vocabulary and agree on one rule: **dependencies point inward**. The domain knows nothing about the database, HTTP, or the UI.

```
Api / Web        ->  depends on Application
Application      ->  depends on Domain
Infrastructure   ->  depends on Domain and Application (implements their interfaces)
Domain           ->  depends on nothing
```

```bash
dotnet new classlib -o src/Shop.Domain
dotnet new classlib -o src/Shop.Application
dotnet new classlib -o src/Shop.Infrastructure
dotnet new web      -o src/Shop.Api
dotnet add src/Shop.Application reference src/Shop.Domain
dotnet add src/Shop.Infrastructure reference src/Shop.Application
dotnet add src/Shop.Api reference src/Shop.Infrastructure
```

The project references are the enforcement mechanism: `Shop.Domain` cannot accidentally use EF Core, because it has no reference to it and the build fails if someone tries.

## Ports and adapters

The application layer declares what it needs; infrastructure supplies it.

```csharp
// Application layer: the port
public interface IOrderRepository
{
    Task<Order?> GetAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct);
    Task SaveAsync(Order order, CancellationToken ct);
}

// Infrastructure layer: the adapter
public sealed class EfOrderRepository(ShopDbContext db) : IOrderRepository
{
    public Task<Order?> GetAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct)
        => db.Orders.Include(o => o.Lines).FirstOrDefaultAsync(o => o.Id == id, ct);

    public async Task SaveAsync(Order order, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        db.Orders.Update(order);
        await db.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
    }
}
```

Swapping SQL Server for Postgres, or the repository for an in-memory fake in tests, is a one-line registration change.

## A domain model with invariants

```csharp
public sealed class Order
{
    private readonly List<OrderLine> _lines = new();
    public Guid Id { get; private set; } = Guid.NewGuid();
    public OrderStatus Status { get; private set; } = OrderStatus.Draft;
    public IReadOnlyList<OrderLine> Lines => _lines;
    public decimal Total => _lines.Sum(l => l.Price * l.Quantity);

    public void AddLine(string sku, int quantity, decimal price)
    {
        if (Status != OrderStatus.Draft)
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Cannot modify a submitted order");
        if (quantity <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(quantity));
        _lines.Add(new OrderLine(sku, quantity, price));
    }

    public void Submit()
    {
        if (_lines.Count == 0) throw new InvalidOperationException("Empty order");
        Status = OrderStatus.Submitted;
    }
}
```

Private setters and a private collection mean there is no way to reach an invalid state from outside. An anemic model with public setters pushes those rules into every service that touches it, and they drift apart.

## CQRS: separating reads from writes

```csharp
public record SubmitOrder(Guid OrderId) : IRequest;

public class SubmitOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repo) : IRequestHandler<SubmitOrder>
{
    public async Task Handle(SubmitOrder cmd, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var order = await repo.GetAsync(cmd.OrderId, ct)
            ?? throw new NotFoundException(cmd.OrderId);
        order.Submit();
        await repo.SaveAsync(order, ct);
    }
}
```

Commands change state and go through the domain model. Queries only read, and are free to bypass the domain entirely with a projection straight to a DTO:

```csharp
var summary = await db.Orders
    .Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId)
    .Select(o => new OrderSummaryDto(o.Id, o.Status, o.Lines.Sum(l => l.Price * l.Quantity)))
    .AsNoTracking()
    .ToListAsync(ct);
```

That is the practical value of CQRS: reads get to be fast and shaped for the screen, writes get to be correct.

> [!NOTE]
> Layers are a cost as well as a benefit. Four projects and a mediator for a CRUD app with six endpoints is ceremony, not architecture. Add the boundary when there is a real reason to defend it: multiple teams, a swappable dependency, or business rules complex enough to be worth isolating.

# Microservices: Resilience, Messaging, and Health

Splitting a system into services replaces method calls with network calls. Networks fail, so resilience stops being optional.

## HTTP clients that survive a bad day

```csharp
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<PricingClient>(c =>
{
    c.BaseAddress = new Uri(builder.Configuration["Services:Pricing"]!);
    c.Timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);
})
.AddStandardResilienceHandler();      // Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience
```

The standard handler bundles the four patterns you would otherwise hand-roll:

| Pattern | What it does | Trap it avoids |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Timeout** | Caps each attempt | A hung dependency exhausting your threads |
| **Retry** with jitter | Retries transient failures | Synchronized retry storms |
| **Circuit breaker** | Stops calling a failing service for a while | Hammering a service that is already down |
| **Rate limiter** | Caps concurrent calls | One slow dependency saturating the pool |

Retry only **idempotent** operations. Retrying `POST /payments` without an idempotency key charges the customer twice.

```csharp
// Idempotency key: the server deduplicates retries of the same logical request
request.Headers.Add("Idempotency-Key", operationId.ToString());
```

Always inject `HttpClient` through `IHttpClientFactory` (`AddHttpClient<T>`). A `new HttpClient()` per request exhausts sockets; one static instance never notices DNS changes.

## Messaging: decoupling in time

```csharp
public record OrderSubmitted(Guid OrderId, string CustomerId, decimal Total, DateTimeOffset At);

await publisher.PublishAsync(new OrderSubmitted(order.Id, customerId, order.Total, now), ct);
```

Synchronous HTTP couples availability: if pricing is down, checkout is down. A queue or broker (RabbitMQ, Azure Service Bus, Kafka) lets the producer continue while consumers catch up.

Consumers must be **idempotent**, because every broker worth using guarantees at-least-once delivery:

```csharp
public async Task HandleAsync(OrderSubmitted evt, CancellationToken ct)
{
    if (await _processed.ContainsAsync(evt.OrderId, ct)) return;   // seen it already
    await _email.SendConfirmationAsync(evt.CustomerId, evt.OrderId, ct);
    await _processed.AddAsync(evt.OrderId, ct);
}
```

The other classic trap is the **dual write**: saving to your database and publishing to the broker are two operations without a shared transaction, so a crash between them loses the event. The outbox pattern fixes it: write the event into an outbox table inside the same database transaction, then have a background worker publish and mark it sent.

## Health checks and graceful shutdown

```csharp
builder.Services.AddHealthChecks()
    .AddDbContextCheck<ShopDbContext>("db")
    .AddUrlGroup(new Uri("https://pricing/health/ready"), "pricing", tags: ["ready"]);

app.MapHealthChecks("/health/live",  new() { Predicate = _ => false });
app.MapHealthChecks("/health/ready", new() { Predicate = c => c.Tags.Contains("ready") });
```

**Liveness** answers "is this process wedged, should the orchestrator restart it?" and must not check dependencies, or one slow database restarts your entire fleet. **Readiness** answers "can this instance serve traffic right now?" and is where dependency checks belong.

```csharp
public class Worker(ILogger<Worker> log) : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            await DoWorkAsync(stoppingToken);
            await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30), stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}
```

Honouring `stoppingToken` is what makes a rolling deploy drain cleanly instead of killing in-flight work.

## Tracing across services

```csharp
builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .WithTracing(t => t.AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation().AddHttpClientInstrumentation().AddOtlpExporter())
    .WithMetrics(m => m.AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation().AddRuntimeInstrumentation().AddOtlpExporter());
```

With W3C trace context propagated automatically across `HttpClient` calls, one trace id follows a request through every service, which is the only practical way to answer "why did this checkout take nine seconds?"

> [!WARNING]
> A retry policy at every layer multiplies: three services each retrying three times turns one user request into 27 calls against the failing dependency. Retry at one well-chosen boundary, and let a circuit breaker cut the traffic when the dependency is genuinely down.

# Security: Authentication, Authorization, and Secrets

**Authentication** is who you are. **Authorization** is what you may do. ASP.NET Core keeps them as separate middleware for exactly that reason.

## JWT bearer authentication

```csharp
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer(options =>
    {
        options.Authority = builder.Configuration["Auth:Authority"];  // the identity provider
        options.Audience  = builder.Configuration["Auth:Audience"];
        options.TokenValidationParameters = new()
        {
            ValidateIssuer = true,
            ValidateAudience = true,
            ValidateLifetime = true,
            ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
            ClockSkew = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30),   // default 5 minutes is generous
        };
    });

app.UseAuthentication();   // establishes HttpContext.User
app.UseAuthorization();    // enforces policies, must come after
```

Setting `Authority` lets the framework fetch and cache the provider's signing keys, so key rotation happens without a deployment. Never disable `ValidateIssuerSigningKey`, and never accept a token whose algorithm header is `none`.

## Authorization policies over scattered role checks

```csharp
builder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("CanRefund", p => p.RequireClaim("permission", "orders:refund"));
    options.AddPolicy("AdultOnly", p => p.RequireAssertion(ctx =>
        ctx.User.FindFirst("birthdate") is { } c &&
        DateTime.TryParse(c.Value, out var d) &&
        d.AddYears(18) <= DateTime.UtcNow));
});

app.MapPost("/orders/{id:guid}/refund", RefundAsync).RequireAuthorization("CanRefund");
app.MapGet("/public/health", () => Results.Ok()).AllowAnonymous();
```

Policies keep the rule in one place. `[Authorize(Roles = "Admin")]` scattered across 40 endpoints is 40 places to update when the rule changes.

Resource-based checks, "may *this* user refund *this* order", belong in an `IAuthorizationHandler`, not in an `if` inside the endpoint.

## Protecting data at rest and in transit

```csharp
builder.Services.AddDataProtection()
    .PersistKeysToFileSystem(new DirectoryInfo("/keys"))   // shared across instances
    .SetApplicationName("Shop");

var protector = provider.CreateProtector("Shop.ResetTokens");
string token = protector.Protect(userId.ToString());
string userIdBack = protector.Unprotect(token);
```

Without persisted, shared keys, every instance generates its own and cookies or tokens issued by one pod are rejected by the next, the classic "logged out on every second request" bug behind a load balancer.

Passwords are hashed, never encrypted:

```csharp
var hasher = new Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.PasswordHasher<User>();
string hash = hasher.HashPassword(user, plaintext);       // PBKDF2, salted, iterated
var result = hasher.VerifyHashedPassword(user, hash, attempt);
```

## The injection surface

```csharp
// SQL injection: EF Core parameterizes LINQ automatically
var safe = await db.Orders.Where(o => o.Customer == input).ToListAsync(ct);

// Raw SQL: interpolation here is parameterized by FromSqlInterpolated, not concatenated
var alsoSafe = await db.Orders
    .FromSqlInterpolated($"SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE Customer = {input}")
    .ToListAsync(ct);

// Never this:
// db.Database.ExecuteSqlRaw("DELETE FROM Orders WHERE Customer = '" + input + "'");
```

Validate input at the boundary, encode output at the point of use, and let the framework parameterize.

## Secrets and headers

```bash
dotnet user-secrets set "Auth:ClientSecret" "dev-only-value"   # development
# production: environment variables, Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager
```

```csharp
app.UseHsts();
app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.Use(async (ctx, next) =>
{
    ctx.Response.Headers["X-Content-Type-Options"] = "nosniff";
    ctx.Response.Headers["Content-Security-Policy"] = "default-src 'self'";
    ctx.Response.Headers["Referrer-Policy"] = "no-referrer";
    await next(ctx);
});

builder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddPolicy("site",
    p => p.WithOrigins("https://kodstigen.se").AllowAnyHeader().AllowAnyMethod()));
```

`AllowAnyOrigin()` combined with credentials is not a CORS configuration, it is a decision to have no origin policy at all.

> [!WARNING]
> Do not log tokens, passwords, connection strings, or full request bodies. Logs get shipped to third-party systems, kept for months, and read by people who never had access to the original credential. Redact at the point of logging, not later.
